/** @page astparserreleasehistory astparser Module Release Notes

    @tableofcontents

    This module is always delivered with %Qore and therefore mirrors the %Qore version.

    @section astparser_3_0 astparser Module Version 3.0
    - added @ref astparser::AstTree::getIdentifierFilter() "AstTree::getIdentifierFilter()" and
      @ref astparser::AstTree::identifierFilterContains() "AstTree::identifierFilterContains()" to test whether an
      identifier can occur in a file without keeping its syntax tree or reading it again
    - a file name with a NUL character is refused instead of being cut at the NUL character:
      @ref astparser::AstParser::parseFile() "AstParser::parseFile()" returns @ref nothing and
      @ref astparser::AstTree::printTree() "AstTree::printTree()" raises \c FILE-OPEN-ERROR
    - supports brace-delimited match, substitution, transliteration and extraction expressions,
      including nested and escaped braces, multiline contents and operation-specific modifiers
    - added @ref astparser::AstTreeSearcher::findScopeSymbols() "AstTreeSearcher::findScopeSymbols()"
      for scope-aware symbol completion
    - @ref astparser::AstTreeSearcher::findSymbols() "AstTreeSearcher::findSymbols()" returns global variables and
      variables of the top-level code, one symbol per declared variable
    - @ref astparser::AstTreeSearcher::resolveDefinition() "AstTreeSearcher::resolveDefinition()" and
      @ref astparser::AstTreeSearcher::findScopeSymbols() "AstTreeSearcher::findScopeSymbols()" support closures,
      including closures in nested hashes and lists: closure parameters and locals are resolved, and the locals of
      the enclosing code are in scope in a closure
    - added \c ADK_Typedef constant for typedef declarations
    - added \c ASYK_TypeAlias constant for typedef symbol kind
    - added \c ASUK_TypedefDeclName and \c ASUK_TypedefTypeName constants for typedef usage kinds
    - fixed off-by-one bug in @ref astparser::AstTree::getNodesInfo() "AstTree::getNodesInfo()" line and column
      numbers (now returns 0-indexed values consistent with LSP convention)
*/
